      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Abstract",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Quantitative Structure–Activity Relationship (QSAR) modeling is a computational approach that establishes mathematical relationships between the chemical structure of compounds and their biological activities",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Bernard R. Brooks et al., 2009)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-dhmyu"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ". By translating molecular structures into numerical descriptors and correlating them with experimental activity data, QSAR models enable the prediction of biological, pharmacological, and toxicological properties of untested compounds. QSAR has become a foundational tool in drug discovery, environmental risk assessment, and regulatory toxicology due to its cost-effectiveness and ability to reduce experimental burden. ",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(David Basketter, 2012)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-wmv34"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " Emphasis is placed on modern machine-learning-based QSAR approaches and the challenges of model interpretability, data quality, and domain applicability.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Nelson Machado & Raúl Domínguez‐Perles, 2017)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-k5ytd"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Keywords:",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " QSAR, cheminformatics, drug discovery, molecular descriptors, predictive modeling",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "horizontalRule"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2,
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "1. Introduction",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "The discovery and development of bioactive compounds traditionally rely on extensive experimental screening, which is time-consuming, expensive, and resource-intensive. Quantitative Structure–Activity Relationship (QSAR) modeling emerged as a solution to predict biological activity from chemical structure alone. The fundamental assumption of QSAR is that ",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "similar chemical structures exhibit similar biological activities",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ", provided they interact with the same biological target.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(David Basketter, 2012)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-4k7pi"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "imageExtension",
      "attrs": {
        "alt": "refill of liquid on tubes",
        "src": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1532187863486-abf9dbad1b69?crop=entropy&cs=tinysrgb&fit=max&fm=jpg&ixid=M3w4MzgzMjh8MHwxfHNlYXJjaHwxMXx8cmVzZWFyY2h8ZW58MHx8fHwxNzcwMDA4NDA2fDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=80&w=1080",
        "align": "left",
        "title": null,
        "width": 436,
        "height": 274,
        "rotate": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "source:",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "https://researchleap.com/developing-a-research-question-the-key-to-successful-research-project/",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "link",
              "attrs": {
                "rel": "noopener noreferrer",
                "href": "https://researchleap.com/developing-a-research-question-the-key-to-successful-research-project/",
                "class": null,
                "target": "_blank"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "Methods convert molecular structures into numerical representations known as molecular descriptors and use statistical or machine learning techniques to establish correlations between these descriptors and observed biological activities. Over the past five decades, QSAR has evolved from simple linear regression models to sophisticated nonlinear and deep learning frameworks, making it a central discipline in computational drug design and toxicolog",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "y",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "d1fc51d9-bebc-4d60-ba10-f08446c7bb95"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " .(Tropsha, 202",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-3ad0q"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "0)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "d1fc51d9-bebc-4d60-ba10-f08446c7bb95"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "horizontalRule"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2,
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "2. Historical Development of QSAR",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "The conceptual foundation of QSAR dates back to the work of Hansch and Fujita in the 1960s, who introduced linear free-energy relationships to correlate physicochemical properties with biological activity. Early QSAR models focused on a limited set of descriptors such as hydrophobicity (logP), electronic effects, and steric parameters.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Flower, 2007)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-7f36a"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Subsequent advances in computational chemistry enabled the generation of thousands of descriptors, leading to the development of multidimensional QSAR (M-QSAR), 3D-QSAR (e.g., CoMFA and CoMSIA), and later machine-learning-based QSAR. Today, QSAR is tightly integrated with cheminformatics, molecular docking, and high-throughput virtual screening pipelines.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Sisheng Ouyang et al., 2012)",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "textStyle",
              "attrs": {
                "fontFamily": "inherit"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "citation",
              "attrs": {
                "citationId": "auto-1770047644006-2s3dw"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "horizontalRule"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2,
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "3. Fundamental Principles of QSAR",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "QSAR modeling is based on three core principles:",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "orderedList",
      "attrs": {
        "type": null,
        "start": 1
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Structure determines activity",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Biological activity is a function of molecular structure and physicochemical properties.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Quantifiable descriptors",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Structural features can be represented numerically using molecular descriptors.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Statistical correlation",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Mathematical models can capture the relationship between descriptors and activity.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Mathematically, a QSAR model can be expressed as:",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "math",
          "attrs": {
            "latex": "Activity=f(D1,D2,D3,…,Dn)Activity = f(D_1, D_2, D_3, \\dots, D_n)Activity=f(D1​,D2​,D3​,…,Dn​)"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "horizontalRule"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "horizontalRule"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2,
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "8. Limitations and Challenges (This Is Where QSAR Breaks)",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Be very clear about this—QSAR is powerful but ",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "text": "fragile if abused",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "orderedList",
      "attrs": {
        "type": null,
        "start": 1
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Garbage in, garbage out",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Poor experimental data guarantees poor models.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Overfitting",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "High accuracy on training data often collapses on new compounds.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Lack of interpretability",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Complex ML models act as black boxes.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Limited extrapolation",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "QSAR cannot reliably predict compounds far outside its training chemical space.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "listItem",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": {
                "textAlign": null
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Biological oversimplification",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hardBreak"
                },
                {
                  "text": "QSAR ignores metabolism, off-target effects, and dynamic biological systems.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "attrs": {
        "textAlign": null
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "If someone claims QSAR alone can replace wet-lab validation—they’re wrong.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
